A shape written under a `boundary` inside a canvas reaches the draw function.

A boundary is how the nodes under it got there rather than a node, the same
thing an `if` and a `for` are -- which is why `emitShapes` and `walkCanvasStmts`
reach through all three. They reached through only the last two, so a shape
under a boundary was dropped from the draw function on every target while its
siblings drew normally, and a canvas under one was never lowered at all.

`testdata/component_treeless_wrapper.sngl` already writes exactly this shape
and is compiled by every platform harness, so the bug was reached on every run
and asserted by nobody. That is what this fixture is for: the circle and the
rect both have to appear, and each target spells them differently enough that a
substring check per target is what says so.

Only Children is walked. `passBoundaryFailed` runs well before `passCanvas` and
has already rewritten the fallback into a reactive `if` inside them, so `Failed`
is empty by the time this runs and walking it would emit the fallback twice.

fyne and gtk4 are absent from the output block on purpose: neither implements
`boundary` at all, and both say so with an error rather than dropping it.

deny out/none/html/index.html `__failed` -- the flag is the fallback's, and this boundary declares no failed slot
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
}

component main() ui.node {
    draw.canvas(width=40px, height=20px) {
        draw.circle(cx=5.0, cy=5.0, r=2.0, style=draw.CanvasStyle{fill=color{r=255}})
        boundary(@error(e) {}) {
            draw.rect(x=0.0, y=0.0, w=4.0, h=4.0, style=draw.CanvasStyle{fill=color{b=255}})
        }
    }
}

ui.window {
    main()
}
